; The hole of a `sub` is a subexpression -- a statement or a term --, never an operator position,
; and a bare operator (`=`, `∧`, a `prop P` with `arity P 2`) is no formula: from `R ((¬¬(=)) c c)`
; "iff-subst" gave `R (c = c)`, the hole cut at the operator `=` of `c = c` (`%b := =`, `%A := R
; (%x c c)`), "not-not" gave `%a := ¬¬=`, and both the search and the kernel read the bare `=` as a
; formula, since its signature says that it yields one -- applied (found 2026-10-16 after soundness
; §8.39; soundness §8.40). Now the hole search skips operator positions (`hole_nodes`), a
; `%`-variable takes no bare operator (`bare_operator_for`), and the kernel reads a bare operator
; as no formula (`k_is_statement`, `k_has_kind`). Since todo 32 (the same day), a bare boolean
; operator where a statement is required is a type error, so the attempts below can't even be
; written any more: they are `TypeError`s at their `use`. The defenses of the search and the kernel
; stay, and are tested with forged certificates (tests/test_kernel.py,
; `test_a_bare_operator_is_no_formula`).
load propositional, equality
const c
prop R 0 1              ; `R` takes a statement
arity R 1
expect "TypeError" "`=` is an operator without its arguments"
    use R ((not (not (=))) c c) "r="

; the same with a `prop` operator of its own
prop P
arity P 2
expect "TypeError" "`P` is an operator without its arguments"
    use R ((not (not P)) c c) "rp"
; and the other way (`%A := S`, `%b := ¬¬S`)
prop S
arity S 2
use R (S c c) "rs"
expect "TypeError" "`S` is an operator without its arguments"
    R ((not (not S)) c c)

; the hole at an argument still works: `%b := P c c`, `%a := ¬¬(P c c)`
use R (not (not (P c c))) "rnn"
R (P c c)

; an operator variable (`var ∘`) matches an operator through the unifier, not through a hole:
; `∘ := plus`, and `rel := sim`, an operator variable with a boolean result
var ∘
infix ∘ 70 70
use ($a ∘ $b) = ($b ∘ $a) "comm"
infix plus 70 70
const plus
const d
(c plus d) = (d plus c)
var rel
infix rel 50 50
prop rel 0
use ($a rel $b) ⇒ ($b rel $a) "rel-sym"
infix sim 50 50
const sim
prop sim 0
(c sim d) ⇒ (d sim c)

; an operator is no object (decided 2026-10-20, doc/kurt-doc.md §5.3): neither a function symbol nor
; a boolean operator is a term on its own, so an equation of function symbols can't be stated
; (from todo 31 to 2026-10-20 it could, and rewrote `f c` to `g c`); a function as a value is a
; mapping of set.kurt (proofs/set-theory/function-extensionality.kurt). Since todo 32 the same
; holds for predicate symbols: `U ⇔ V` with `arity U 1` is a type error
const f, g
arity f 1, g 1
expect "TypeError" "`f` is an operator without its arguments"
    use f = g "fg"
prop U, V
arity U 1, V 1
expect "TypeError" "`U` is an operator without its arguments"
    use U ⇔ V "uv"
; (stated for their arguments, it does: `∀ x (U x ⇔ V x)`, with `load logic`)
